Title: The Playboy of the Western World, Act 2 (the looking-glass)
Author: J. M. Synge
Source: Project Gutenberg ebook 1240, lines 1160 to 1300

INT. SHEBEEN 1 - DAY

CHRISTY
Half a hundred beyond. Ten there. A score that’s above. Eighty jugs. Six cups and a broken one. Two plates. A power of glasses. Bottles, a school-master’d be hard set to count, and enough in them, I’m thinking, to drunken all the wealth and wisdom of the County Clare. There’s her boots now, nice and decent for her evening use, and isn’t it grand brushes she has? Well, this’d be a fine place to be my whole life talking out with swearing Christians, in place of my old dogs and cat, and I stalking around, smoking my pipe and drinking my fill, and never a day’s work but drawing a cork an odd time, or wiping a glass, or rinsing out a shiny tumbler for a decent man. Didn’t I know rightly I was handsome, though it was the divil’s own mirror we had beyond, would twist a squint across an angel’s brow; and I’ll be growing fine from this day, the way I’ll have a soft lovely skin on me and won’t be the like of the clumsy young fellows do be ploughing all times in the earth and dung. Is she coming again? Stranger girls. God help me, where’ll I hide myself away and my long neck naked to the world? I’d best go to the room maybe till I’m dressed again.

!to himself, counting jugs on dresser.

!He gathers up his coat and the looking-glass, and runs into the inner room. The door is pushed open, and Susan Brady looks in, and knocks on door.

!He puts down the boot carefully.

!He puts them down and goes by degrees to the looking-glass.

!He takes the looking-glass from the wall and puts it on the back of a chair; then sits down in front of it and begins washing his face.

!He starts.

!He looks out.

!He looks out.

SUSAN
There’s nobody in it.

!Knocks again.

NELLY
It’d be early for them both to be out walking the hill.

!pushing her in and following her, with Honor Blake and Sara Tansey.

SUSAN
I’m thinking Shawn Keogh was making game of us and there’s no such man in it at all.

HONOR
Look at that. He’s been sleeping there in the night. Well, it’ll be a hard case if he’s gone off now, the way we’ll never set our eyes on a man killed his father, and we after rising early and destroying ourselves running fast on the hill.

!pointing to straw and quilt.

NELLY
Are you thinking them’s his boots?

INT. SHEBEEN 2 - DAY

SARA
If they are, there should be his father’s track on them. Did you never read in the papers the way murdered men do bleed and drip?

!taking them up.

SUSAN
Is that blood there, Sara Tansey?

SARAH
That’s bog water, I’m thinking, but it’s his own they are surely, for I never seen the like of them for whity mud, and red mud, and turf on them, and the fine sands of the sea. That man’s been walking, I’m telling you.

!smelling it.

!She goes down right, putting on one of his boots.

SUSAN
Maybe he’s stolen off to Belmullet with the boots of Michael James, and you’d have a right so to follow after him, Sara Tansey, and you the one yoked the ass cart and drove ten miles to set your eyes on the man bit the yellow lady’s nostril on the northern shore.

!going to window.

!She looks out.

SARA
Don’t be talking, and we fooled to-day. There’s a pair do fit me well, and I’ll be keeping them for walking to the priest, when you’d be ashamed this place, going up winter and summer with nothing worth while to confess at all.

!running to window with one boot on.

!Putting on other boot.

HONOR
Whisht! there’s someone inside the room. It’s a man.

!who has been listening at the door.

!She pushes door a chink open.

!Sara kicks off boots and puts them where they were. They all stand in a line looking through chink.

INT. SHEBEEN 3 - DAY

SARA
I’ll call him. Mister! Mister! Is Pegeen within?

!He puts in his head.

CHRISTY
She’s above on the cnuceen, seeking the nanny goats, the way she’d have a sup of goat’s milk for to colour my tea.

!coming in as meek as a mouse, with the looking-glass held behind his back.

SARA
And asking your pardon, is it you’s the man killed his father?

CHRISTY
I am, God help me!

!sidling toward the nail where the glass was hanging.

SARA
Then my thousand welcomes to you, and I’ve run up with a brace of duck’s eggs for your food today. Pegeen’s ducks is no use, but these are the real rich sort. Hold out your hand and you’ll see it’s no lie I’m telling you.

!taking eggs she has brought.

CHRISTY
They’re a great and weighty size.

!coming forward shyly, and holding out his left hand.

INT. SHEBEEN 4 - DAY

SUSAN
And I run up with a pat of butter, for it’d be a poor thing to have you eating your spuds dry, and you after running a great way since you did destroy your da.

CHRISTY
Thank you kindly.

HONOR
And I brought you a little cut of cake, for you should have a thin stomach on you, and you that length walking the world.

NELLY
And I brought you a little laying pullet—boiled and all she is—was crushed at the fall of night by the curate’s car. Feel the fat of that breast, Mister.

CHRISTY
It’s bursting, surely.

!He feels it with the back of his hand, in which he holds the presents.

SARA
Will you pinch it? Is your right hand too sacred for to use at all? It’s a glass he has. Well, I never seen to this day a man with a looking-glass held to his back. Them that kills their fathers is a vain lot surely.

!She slips round behind him.

!Girls giggle.

INT. SHEBEEN 5 - DAY

CHRISTY
I’m very thankful to you all to-day....

!smiling innocently and piling presents on glass.

WIDOW QUIN
Sara Tansey, Susan Brady, Honor Blake! What in glory has you here at this hour of day?

!coming in quickly, at door.

GIRLS
That’s the man killed his father.

!giggling.
